WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.380
The first film left the blue and gold allele bar unchanged after mating.

2
00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:07.520
What must happen to move that boundary?

3
00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:12.860
Keep following copies as different processes act on the population.

4
00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:16.620
The answer depends on what touches those copies.

5
00:00:20.183 --> 00:00:24.863
Selection acts when genotypes leave different numbers of surviving offspring.

6
00:00:26.223 --> 00:00:29.783
Suppose a genotype contributes fewer copies to the next pool.

7
00:00:31.003 --> 00:00:34.983
The bar moves predictably toward the copies contributed more often.

8
00:00:36.203 --> 00:00:39.683
Which genotype has that advantage depends on the environment.

9
00:00:40.783 --> 00:00:43.723
So the direction can change when the environment changes.

10
00:00:44.917 --> 00:00:48.097
Migration moves copies between populations.

11
00:00:49.477 --> 00:00:54.337
Pour a small stream from a population with a different allele mix into the resident

12
00:00:54.337 --> 00:00:58.637
pool. The new frequency becomes a weighted average.

13
00:00:59.857 --> 00:01:01.537
It shifts toward the migrants' mix.

14
00:01:02.797 --> 00:01:05.457
The amount of movement depends on the migrants' share.

15
00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:09.500
Mutation changes the identity of a copy.

16
00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:13.760
A blue block flips to gold as it enters the next pool.

17
00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:17.420
That creates a new route between allele states.

18
00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:22.620
At a human locus, the immediate frequency change is usually weak.

19
00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:27.480
Mutation supplies variation on which other forces act.

20
00:01:28.533 --> 00:01:31.153
Now copy the same small population twice.

21
00:01:32.573 --> 00:01:35.413
Each copy samples its next generation by chance.

22
00:01:36.673 --> 00:01:38.813
Both begin with the same copy inventory.

23
00:01:40.453 --> 00:01:41.453
Predict the result.

24
00:01:42.513 --> 00:01:47.833
Must both allele bars move together, or can identical starting populations separate

25
00:01:47.833 --> 00:01:48.833
without selection?

26
00:01:54.817 --> 00:01:56.037
They can separate.

27
00:01:57.677 --> 00:02:01.317
Drift is random sampling of allele copies between generations.

28
00:02:02.737 --> 00:02:06.457
One sample happens to take more blue; another takes more gold.

29
00:02:07.957 --> 00:02:12.637
Across many repeats there is no preferred direction, but small populations spread

30
00:02:12.637 --> 00:02:13.637
widely.

31
00:02:14.167 --> 00:02:17.287
Non-random mating produces a different signature.

32
00:02:18.787 --> 00:02:23.267
Relatives, or partners alike in a trait this locus affects, pair more often.

33
00:02:24.627 --> 00:02:27.847
So homozygotes can increase while heterozygotes decrease.

34
00:02:29.227 --> 00:02:31.267
Yet every blue and gold copy remains.

35
00:02:32.547 --> 00:02:34.627
The bar therefore stays in the same place.

36
00:02:35.987 --> 00:02:41.447
Mating pattern alone changes genotype frequencies, not allele frequencies.

37
00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:46.590
These processes can overlap, so an unusual sample rarely names a cause.

38
00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:53.290
A heterozygote deficit might reflect non-random mating, mixed populations, or

39
00:02:53.290 --> 00:02:58.210
genotyping error. A pattern can therefore support several explanations.

40
00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:04.430
Population genetics compares predictions with patterns, then asks for independent

41
00:03:04.430 --> 00:03:05.430
evidence.

42
00:03:06.083 --> 00:03:07.923
Track what each process touches.

43
00:03:09.743 --> 00:03:11.023
Selection changes contribution.

44
00:03:12.583 --> 00:03:14.143
Migration changes membership.

45
00:03:15.563 --> 00:03:17.243
Mutation changes identity.

46
00:03:18.823 --> 00:03:19.923
Drift changes the sample.

47
00:03:21.343 --> 00:03:22.923
Non-random mating changes pairing.

48
00:03:24.183 --> 00:03:28.163
The moving bar is evidence to explain, not a label for one cause.

49
00:03:29.267 --> 00:03:33.187
Forces change allele frequencies in distinct, testable ways.

50
00:03:34.527 --> 00:03:39.067
Follow the copies, compare the expected signature with the data, and keep

51
00:03:39.067 --> 00:03:42.767
alternative causes open until more evidence separates them.
